The technical bug is simple. What makes this case worth studying is what regulators found afterwards.

In July 2020 the New York State Department of Financial Services (NYDFS, the state's banking and insurance regulator) [filed charges](https://www.dfs.ny.gov/reports_and_publications/press_releases/pr202007221), the first under its Cybersecurity Regulation (23 NYCRR Part 500: Part 500 of Title 23 of the New York Codes, Rules and Regulations). It alleged that an **internal penetration test** (an authorized simulated attack by the company's own testers) had found the exposure in **December 2018**, five months before Krebs's story, and that First American then:

- misclassified it as **"low" severity** despite the size of the exposure;
- reviewed only **10** of the millions of exposed documents, grossly underestimating it;
- did not investigate within the time its own policy required, and did not follow its security team's recommendation to investigate further.

